og in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 11 Nov 2025]\nL9: # Title:BayesQ: Uncertainty-Guided Bayesian Quantization\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Ismail Lamaakal , cite6†Chaymae Yahyati , cite7†Yassine Maleh , cite8†Khalid El Makkaoui , cite9†Ibrahim Ouahbi L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled BayesQ: Uncertainty-Guided Bayesian Quantization, by Ismail Lamaakal and 4 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:We present BayesQ, an uncertainty-guided post-training quantization framework that is the first to optimize quantization under the posterior expected loss. BayesQ fits a lightweight Gaussian posterior over weights (diagonal Laplace by default; optional K-FAC/low-rank), whitens by the posterior covariance, designs codebooks to minimize posterior-expected distortion, and allocates mixed precision via a greedy knapsack that maximizes marginal expected-loss reduction per bit under a global budget.\nL17: For scalar quantizers, posterior-expected MSE yields closed-form tables; task-aware proxies are handled by short Monte Carlo on a small calibration set. An optional calibration-only distillation aligns the quantized model with the posterior predictive teacher. At matched average bits/weight of 3.0/3.5/4.0, BayesQ improves over strong PTQ baselines on ResNet-50 (ImageNet) and BERT-base (GLUE) e.g., vs.\nL18: GPTQ by $+1.5/+0.7/+0.3$ top-1 percentage points on RN50 and $+1.1/+0.4/+0.2$ GLUE points on BERT, while requiring one-time preprocessing comparable to a GPTQ pass. BayesQ reframes low-bit quantization as uncertainty-aware risk minimization in a practical, post-training pipeline.\nL19: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2511.08821 [cs.LG]\nL21:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2511.08821v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL22:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.08821†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Ismail Lamaakal Dr. [cite15†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Tue, 11 Nov 2025 22:36:19 UTC (692 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled BayesQ: Uncertainty-Guided Bayesian Quantization, by Ismail Lamaakal and 4 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * cite10†View PDF L35:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L36:   * cite16†TeX Source L37: \nL38: cite17†view license†creativecommons.org L39: ### Current browse context:\nL40: \nL41: cs.LG\nL42: \nL43: cite18†< prev |   cite19†next > L44: \nL45: cite20†new | cite21†recent | cite22†2025-11 L46: \nL47: Change to browse by:\nL48: \nL49: cite23†cs L50: cite24†cs.CL L51: cite25†cs.CV L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite26†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite27†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite28†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL75: \nL76: Bibliographic Explorer (cite29†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL77: \nL78: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL79: \nL80: Connected Papers (cite30†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL81: \nL82: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL83: \nL84: Litmaps (cite31†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL85: \nL86: [Input] scite.ai Toggle\nL87: \nL88: scite Smart Citations (cite32†What are Smart Citations?†www.scite.ai )\nL89: \nL90: [