aced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nL157: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? cite45†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org .\nL158: \nL159: cite46†Which authors of this paper are endorsers? | Disable MathJax (cite47†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org )\nL160: \nL161: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite48†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL162: cite49†About†info.arxiv.org · cite50†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite51†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite52†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite53†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite54†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite55†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite56†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L163: \nL164: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2511.08914] SPEED-Q: Staged Processing with Enhanced Distillation towards Efficient Low-bit On-device VLM Quantization (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08914)\nciteturn5914view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.08914\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Nov 2025]\nL9: # Title:SPEED-Q: Staged Processing with Enhanced Distillation towards Efficient Low-bit On-device VLM Quantization\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Tianyu Guo , cite6†Shanwei Zhao , cite7†Shiai Zhu , cite8†Chenguang Ma L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled SPEED-Q: Staged Processing with Enhanced Distillation towards Efficient Low-bit On-device VLM Quantization, by Tianyu Guo and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite9†View PDF cite10†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Deploying Vision-Language Models (VLMs) on edge devices (e.g., smartphones and robots) is crucial for enabling low-latency and privacy-preserving intelligent applications. Given the resource constraints of these devices, quantization offers a promising solution by improving memory efficiency and reducing bandwidth requirements, thereby facilitating the deployment of VLMs.\nL17: However, existing research has rarely explored aggressive quantization on VLMs, particularly for the models ranging from 1B to 2B parameters, which are more suitable for resource-constrained edge devices.\nL18: In this paper, we propose SPEED-Q, a novel Staged Processing with Enhanced Distillation framework for VLM low-bit weight-only quantization that systematically addresses the following two critical obstacles: (1) significant discrepancies in quantization sensitivity between vision (ViT) and language (LLM) components in VLMs; (2) training instability arising from the reduced numerical precision inherent in low-bit quantization.\nL19: In SPEED-Q, a staged sensitivity adaptive mechanism is introduced to effectively harmonize performance across different modalities. We further propose a distillation-enhanced quantization strategy to stabilize the training process and reduce data dependence. Together, SPEED-Q enables accurate, stable, and data-efficient quantization of complex VLMs. SPEED-Q is the first framework tailored for quantizing entire small-scale billion-parameter VLMs to low bits.\nL20: Extensive experiments across multiple benchmarks demonstrate that SPEED-Q achieves up to 6x higher accuracy than existing quantization methods under 2-bit settings and consistently outperforms prior on-device VLMs under both 2-bit and 4-bit settings. Our code and models are available at cite11†this https URL†github.com .\nL21: Subjects:  | Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2511.08914 [cs.CV]\nL23:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2511.08914v1 [cs.CV] for this version)\nL24:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.08914†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Tianyu Guo [cite15†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Wed, 12 Nov 2025 02:47:24 UTC (1,958 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled SPEED-Q: Staged Processing with Enhanced Distillation towards Efficient Low-bit On-device VLM Quantization, by Tianyu Guo and 3 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite9†View PDF L37:   * cite10†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite16†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite17†view license L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.CV\nL44: \nL45: cite18†< prev |   cite19†next > L46: \nL47: cite20†new | cite21†recent | cite22†2025-11 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite23†cs L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite24†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite25†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite26†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL75: \nL76: Bibliographic Explorer (cite27†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL77: \nL78: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL79: \nL80: Connected Papers (cite28†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL81: \nL82: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL83: \nL84: Litmaps (cite29†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL85: \nL86: [Input] scite.ai Toggle\nL87: \nL88: scite Smart Cit